were by themselves and when there was a caregiver. And, you know, it doesn't take much. Like with babies that are about three months of age, our first study found that about 50% of three-month-olds would smile simply in response to a caregiver's smile. But by six months, only about five percent of babies would do that. So smiling alone is not quite enough. There is this cognitive piece that is involved a little bit later. But you're asking a really insightful question, which is that, yes, smiling does seem to be this social signal so that if you're by yourself, you know, you might as well reserve your smiling. What if a baby doesn't start laughing around five months or so? What does that indicate?

