37
00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:45.000
So from that point on, I was never concerned. And the work has been wonderfully received everywhere that I've presented it, or my students have presented it. In terms of getting funding, I will say that I don't use the words humor. I don't use. We do say that we measure laughter. But, you know, it's what we're really studying was something you were getting at earlier on in this conversation, which is that we're studying something cognitive here. So we're interested in this cognitive skill and whether or not infants have it. And that is

38
00:23:45.000 --> 00:24:20.000
really their ability to detect incongruity, which means an unusual or unexpected event, and to appraise it. And what are they using in their environment to appraise it? So that's the language that we use in funding. So what are you studying now? What are the big questions that you're still trying to answer? Yeah. So we just finished up a study. We have manuscript in revision. Fingers crossed. That's all done pretty soon. One of the questions that I was really intrigued by was how infants differentiate

39
00:24:20.000 --> 00:25:18.000
different types of incongruities. So there's a big body of literature that looks at magical incongruities. These are impossible events like a ball floating in the air, for example, or an object turning into another object. So we call them magical because these are kind of impossible events. And there's a whole wonderful body of literature that shows that starting at about three and a half months of age, babies stare at those events when you compare those to control events. So they're really captivated by them. And then in my line of research, which is like four people doing this work, we show babies incongruities, but they're silly incongruities. So the question that we've just gone after is how do babies distinguish these types of events, you know, can they? And what we did in this last study was we presented babies with magical incongruities, but we put them in different contexts.

40
00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:48.000
In the magic literature, babies just see the events. They don't see a person. They just see hands or they see a theater or a screen where the events are unfolding in an asocial context. So all we did was take two magic tricks and we put them in a social context. We had a magician perform the events. They did not interact with the babies. And babies were much more likely to smile at these events. They actually did not look longer at these events, which contradicts this other body of literature.

41
00:25:48.000 --> 00:26:18.000
But it's one study. It's in a naturalistic context. It needs to be replicated. But it is interesting to think that, you know, infants are really, really picking on the social environment and that they're deriving meaning about events just based on the presence of another person. And it could be, it could be that they're attributing intentionality, like, oh, that person meant to do that. And that lets them resolve the incongruity, which is like getting the punchline. Like, oh, that's what you meant.

42
00:26:18.000 --> 00:27:04.000
That's funny, you know. So I'm just working on our next study with a brilliant colleague, John Lane, at Vanderbilt. He's been a consultant on this current study. And we're thinking about putting this question of intentionality really to the test and showing infants magic tricks where the magician will use a technique that, again, Elena Hoika at the UK Bristol University has used, where infants hear one of two verbal cues, they either hear the magician say, uh-oh, like this was a mistake or, ha-ha, like I meant to do that, and seeing if infants can use those cues to understand the intentionality of the magician in one condition, and whether or not

43
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:26.000
they'll be more likely to laugh in response or smile. So Elena's done something like that with older toddlers. So we'd really be scaling it back for babies. Well, Dr. Moreau, I want to thank you for joining me today. This was really a lot of fun and got me laughing too. Oh, great, great. Well, that's always a great success. Thank you so much for your interest.

